 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:58:49.337167Z",
 "text": "**Verdict: enforceable.** The clause requires a rent deposit of no more than one month's rent, applied to the last month's rent. Ontario permits that arrangement.\n\nUnder s. 106 of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*, a landlord may require a rent deposit, but it cannot exceed one month's rent for the unit. This clause is set at exactly one month, so it falls within the limit. Taking the deposit at signing and applying it to the last month's rent is also consistent with how the Act treats rent deposits. Other rules still apply separately. The landlord must pay interest on the deposit under s. 107, and the deposit cannot be used for other purposes. Neither of those makes the clause itself unenforceable.\n\nI cite s. 106 from memory and have not checked the current consolidation, so confirm the section number against the official text on e-Laws before relying on it.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 106",
